[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the inductive step more concretely. I'll try to show that we [0m[38;2;124;124;124mcan reduce an (n+3)×(n+3) block to an n×n block.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe idea: The 3-wide frame around the n×n block can be "pushed inward" by using [0m[38;2;124;124;124mjumps that convert 3 rows of the frame into modifications of the inner block.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, here's a cleaner approach. Let me think about reducing a 3×2 block (3 [0m[38;2;124;124;124mrows, 2 columns) to a single piece at a specific position.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m3×2 block at (0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1),(2,0),(2,1). Invariant: colors (i+j) mod 3:[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(0,1),(1,2),(2,0). a=2,b=2,c=2. (a+c,b+c)=(4,4)=(0,0). Can't reduce to 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWhat about a 3×4 block? 12 pieces. Colors: rows 0,1,2 (residues 0,1,2), columns [0m[38;2;124;124;124m0,1,2,3 (residues 0,1,2,0). [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mRow 0: colors 0,1,2,0 → a=2,b=1,c=1[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mRow 1: colors 1,2,0,1 → a=1,b=2,c=1[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mRow 2: colors 2,0,1,2 → a=1,b=1,c=2[0m
