[38;2;124;124;124m11. (2,4)→(0,4): vertical, removes (1,4)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m12. (0,5)→(0,3): horizontal, removes (0,4)[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m13. (0,3)→(2,3): vertical, removes (1,3)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe pattern seems to be:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. Create pieces in row 2 by vertical jumps from row 0.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. Use horizontal jumps in row 2 to consolidate.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. Use vertical jumps to move pieces back to row 0.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. Use horizontal jumps in row 0 to consolidate.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m5. Repeat until 1 piece remains.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a "zigzag" pattern between rows 0 and 2 (using row 1 as the "jumped-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mover" row).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key insight is that vertical jumps from row 0 over row 1 to row 2 (and back)[0m[38;2;124;124;124mallow the pieces to alternate between two rows, effectively doubling the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mavailable space for horizontal jumps.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis zigzag pattern might generalize to arbitrary n. Let me think about how.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn the 2×n strip, the two rows are at rows 0 and 1. Vertical jumps create pieces[0m[38;2;124;124;124mat rows -1 and 2 (just outside the strip). Horizontal jumps in rows -1, 0, 1, 2 [0m[38;2;124;124;124mallow consolidation.[0m

