[38;2;124;124;124mI keep running into the same issue: after processing a chunk, the remnants are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mat distance 2 with an empty position between them, preventing further jumps.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe root cause is that each jump moves a piece by 2, creating a gap of 2 between[0m[38;2;124;124;124mpieces. After consolidation, the pieces are at even positions, with odd [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpositions empty. This is the "parity" issue in 1D peg solitaire.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn 1D, the parity issue prevents reduction when the number of pieces is ≡ 0 mod [0m[38;2;124;124;124m3. In 2D, the vertical jumps provide a way to break the parity, but it requires [0m[38;2;124;124;124mcareful coordination.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me look at the explicit 2×4 solution again to understand how it overcomes [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe parity issue.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m2×4 solution:[0m
